1
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:40,340
A flammable can be simply described as a substance that will readily catch fire and burn.

2
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:43,840
Xylene is a commonly used flammable substance.

3
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:48,840
There can be two identical amounts sitting side by side in identical dishes.

4
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:52,840
Yet one will ignite, the other one won't.

5
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:57,340
The reason? The xylene that didn't ignite was cooled by dry ice.

6
00:00:57,340 --> 00:01:02,340
The other was heated to just over 81 degrees, the flashpoint of xylene.

7
00:01:02,340 --> 00:01:09,340
Flashpoint is the lowest temperature at which a substance gives off vapor that will burn.

8
00:01:09,340 --> 00:01:13,340
One of the first things you need to remember when you're dealing with flammables and explosives

9
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:20,340
is that it's the vapor that a substance gives off that burns, not the substance itself.

10
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:26,340
Flammables and explosives are two classes of materials that can always be dangerous.

11
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:33,840
While flammables can ignite and burn, explosives are subject to very rapid chemical reaction or decomposition

12
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:39,840
and can release gas and heat with potentially violent results.

13
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:58,610
Flammables and explosives are both materials that you want to be very careful with.

14
00:01:58,610 --> 00:02:02,610
Let's look at another highly flammable material, gasoline.

15
00:02:02,610 --> 00:02:08,610
Its vapors ignite easily at temperatures as low as negative 45 degrees Fahrenheit.

16
00:02:08,610 --> 00:02:14,110
Even when it's at its flashpoint and a spark is created, gasoline may not catch fire.

17
00:02:14,110 --> 00:02:20,110
Whether it does will depend on the gasoline's fuel-air mixture and limits of flammability.

18
00:02:20,110 --> 00:02:24,110
Materials have both lower and upper limits of flammability.

19
00:02:24,110 --> 00:02:30,110
A substance's lower flammable limit is the minimum percentage of vapor to air

20
00:02:30,110 --> 00:02:33,110
that's required for ignition to take place.

21
00:02:33,110 --> 00:02:41,110
The upper flammable limit is the percentage of vapor to air beyond which ignition is no longer possible.

22
00:02:41,110 --> 00:02:50,610
For example, the limits of flammability for diethyl ether range from 1.9% to 36.5% by volume of air.

23
00:02:50,610 --> 00:02:55,610
This means it will ignite if the atmosphere is only 1.9% ether,

24
00:02:55,610 --> 00:03:03,370
and it can't ignite if the atmosphere contains more than 36.5% ether.

25
00:03:03,370 --> 00:03:12,180
Remember, most lower limits are reached quickly, so it can be very easy for a fire or explosion to erupt in a lab.

26
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:14,180
And keep this in mind.

27
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:18,180
If there is enough vapor in the air to create a flammable atmosphere,

28
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:24,120
the atmosphere will usually be above toxic limits as well.

29
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:28,120
In addition to having their flammability limits affect when they will burn,

30
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:31,620
all flammables also have an ignition temperature.

31
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:36,120
This is the minimum temperature that is required to cause self-contained combustion,

32
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:40,120
regardless of the heat source that is involved.

33
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:45,120
Carbon disulfide, for instance, has an ignition temperature of 80 degrees centigrade.

34
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:50,120
As a result, it can easily ignite via a nearby light bulb or steam line.

35
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:57,530
Similarly, the surface temperature of a hot plate will ignite diethyl ether.

36
00:03:57,530 --> 00:04:01,030
So when you're working with flammable and explosive materials,

37
00:04:01,030 --> 00:04:06,530
you should remove equipment that will cause heat or sparks from your work area if at all possible.

38
00:04:06,530 --> 00:04:11,030
Even things like stirrers and pumps can cause problems.

39
00:04:11,030 --> 00:04:15,030
And never use an open flame or a hot plate to heat flammables.

40
00:04:15,030 --> 00:04:20,030
Instead, use a heating mantle or water bath.

41
00:04:20,030 --> 00:04:24,030
Static electricity, which can be generated just by walking across a rug,

42
00:04:24,030 --> 00:04:27,530
is another thing that you need to be concerned about.

43
00:04:27,530 --> 00:04:31,030
So whenever you can, be sure to use static guard devices

44
00:04:31,030 --> 00:04:35,500
when you're working with flammables and explosives.

45
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:40,500
If you have any questions, consult the safety data sheets of the chemicals that you're using

46
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:57,570
for information on the flashpoints, ignition temperatures and flammability limits.

47
00:04:57,570 --> 00:05:02,570
Many gases that are used in laboratories can be both flammable and explosive,

48
00:05:02,570 --> 00:05:06,570
so it's important to know how to store gas cylinders as well.

49
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:11,070
Strap or chain flammable gas cylinders securely to benches or wall racks

50
00:05:11,070 --> 00:05:15,070
so they don't fall and knock valves open or break hose lines.

51
00:05:15,070 --> 00:05:20,070
Fittings and hoses should be checked regularly to see if they've developed any leaks.

52
00:05:20,070 --> 00:05:25,070
Don't store gases that could react next to one another.

53
00:05:25,070 --> 00:05:29,070
Maintaining the correct labeling on a cylinder is important as well.

54
00:05:29,070 --> 00:05:33,070
You can't rely on the paint color to tell you what's in it.

55
00:05:33,070 --> 00:05:37,570
Many suppliers don't adhere to a strict color code.

56
00:05:37,570 --> 00:05:42,570
When you take a cylinder out of storage, check all connections for leaks once again.

57
00:05:42,570 --> 00:05:46,070
Use a dilute soap solution at fittings and valves.

58
00:05:46,070 --> 00:05:51,020
Bubbles will tell you if a leak exists.

59
00:05:51,020 --> 00:05:54,020
In addition to sharing some characteristics with flammables,

60
00:05:54,020 --> 00:05:58,520
explosives have unique attributes of their own.

61
00:05:58,520 --> 00:06:02,020
Many of them are affected dramatically by increases in temperature.

62
00:06:02,020 --> 00:06:06,020
They will decompose quickly or become dangerously reactive.

63
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:11,020
Light, mechanical shock and certain catalysts can also start a reaction.

64
00:06:11,020 --> 00:06:16,020
You'll also need to be concerned about mixtures when you think about explosives.

65
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:20,020
Some chemicals form an explosive compound when they're brought together,

66
00:06:20,020 --> 00:06:24,020
such as calcium carbide and water.

67
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:27,020
Impurities in a sample can be dangerous as well.

68
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:31,020
Organic peroxides can form on containers of diethyl ether.

69
00:06:31,020 --> 00:06:35,020
These peroxides are notoriously unstable and explosive.

70
00:06:35,020 --> 00:06:41,020
They're also highly flammable and far more susceptible to shock than even dynamite.

71
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:48,020
Something as simple as the friction caused by opening a contaminated jar can cause an explosion.

72
00:06:48,020 --> 00:06:55,040
To make sure that you're familiar with any of the problems that could surface with these substances,

73
00:06:55,040 --> 00:07:00,040
check your safety data sheets and other guides before you handle or store them.

74
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:18,040
Ventilation is particularly important when you're working with flammable and explosive chemicals.

75
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:21,040
You must have enough ventilation to keep the vapor in the air

76
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:24,040
from reaching your material's lower flammable limits.

77
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:31,540
Working under a hood can help to confine and remove flammable and toxic vapors.

78
00:07:31,540 --> 00:07:34,540
Local ventilation controls also help prevent flashback,

79
00:07:34,540 --> 00:07:39,540
the accidental reverse flow of flame back to a container.

80
00:07:39,540 --> 00:07:45,540
But in some cases, you'll need to use auxiliary ventilation when working with flammables and explosives.

81
00:07:45,540 --> 00:07:53,040
How you transport flammable and explosive materials is also critical.

82
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:56,040
They should always be carried in secondary containers.

83
00:07:56,040 --> 00:07:59,040
Rubber acid carriers are a good choice.

84
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:04,410
Plastic jacket overpacks also work well.

85
00:08:04,410 --> 00:08:09,410
If you need to cross any uneven surfaces, be sure to use carts with large wheels.

86
00:08:09,410 --> 00:08:12,410
Don't use instrument, equipment, or mail carts.

87
00:08:12,410 --> 00:08:17,410
They're too easy to tip over, and chemicals can easily be knocked off of them too.

88
00:08:17,410 --> 00:08:25,010
Every chemical should have a specific storage place where it's returned to after each use.

89
00:08:25,010 --> 00:08:28,010
Flammables and explosives are no exception.

90
00:08:28,010 --> 00:08:32,010
Avoid locations that have exposure to heat or direct sunlight.

91
00:08:32,010 --> 00:08:36,010
Flammable liquids should be stored in approved safety containers.

92
00:08:36,010 --> 00:08:41,010
Red containers with yellow tape identify substances having low flashpoints.

93
00:08:41,010 --> 00:08:45,010
Arrestor spouts help prevent flashback.

94
00:08:45,010 --> 00:08:47,010
Be sure to label the containers.

95
00:08:47,010 --> 00:08:52,010
You don't want any confusion where flammables are concerned.

96
00:08:52,010 --> 00:08:56,010
Storing your chemicals on a benchtop is not a good idea.

97
00:08:56,010 --> 00:09:02,010
It's too easy to knock them over, and on a bench they're not protected from fire.

98
00:09:02,010 --> 00:09:05,010
Inhood storage is also unadvisable.

99
00:09:05,010 --> 00:09:08,010
The containers interfere with the airflow in the hood,

100
00:09:08,010 --> 00:09:14,290
and the chemicals increase the number of materials that could be involved in a hood fire.

101
00:09:14,290 --> 00:09:19,290
Amounts of flammable and explosive chemicals exceeding one liter in volume

102
00:09:19,290 --> 00:09:22,290
should be stored in approved flammables cabinets.

103
00:09:22,290 --> 00:09:28,290
The cabinets don't have to be vented, but if they are, the vent pipe should have a flame arrestor.

104
00:09:28,290 --> 00:09:34,290
In some areas, local building codes may prohibit venting cabinets into the outside air.

105
00:09:34,290 --> 00:09:38,290
Keep cabinet doors closed and locked.

106
00:09:38,290 --> 00:09:46,290
Never store flammables or explosives in a lab refrigerator unless it's been certified as explosion proof.

107
00:09:46,290 --> 00:09:50,290
The National Fire Protection Association and your facility's policies

108
00:09:50,290 --> 00:09:57,290
regulate how much flammable and explosive material can be stored within lab areas.

109
00:09:57,290 --> 00:10:02,290
You should consult your supervisor to determine what the permitted storage levels are.

110
00:10:02,290 --> 00:10:22,440
Safe work practices also play an important role when you're using flammables and explosives.

111
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:28,440
When you're working with them, use the smallest amounts possible to attain the desired reaction.

112
00:10:28,440 --> 00:10:33,440
If there's a potential for explosion, place your equipment behind a blast shield.

113
00:10:33,440 --> 00:10:36,440
Wear a face shield and appropriate gloves.

114
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:41,440
You should also use a lab coat that's made from a slow burning material.

115
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:46,440
Make frequent checks of stored containers. Look for evidence of corrosion or contamination.

116
00:10:46,440 --> 00:10:50,440
Push containers back from shelf edges if they've gotten too close.

117
00:10:50,440 --> 00:10:57,160
Disposing of waste from flammable and explosive materials can be tricky.

118
00:10:57,160 --> 00:11:01,160
This is another place where you should consult safety data sheets.

119
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:04,160
Be sure to take all the appropriate precautions.

120
00:11:04,160 --> 00:11:09,990
Very few of these materials can safely go down the drain.

121
00:11:09,990 --> 00:11:15,990
Generally, water treatment plants cannot filter out toxic substances like flammables.

122
00:11:15,990 --> 00:11:22,990
If you are permitted to use drain disposal, flammable should always be diluted so they don't pose a fire hazard.

123
00:11:22,990 --> 00:11:26,990
Simple reactions inside pipes can cause explosions.

124
00:11:26,990 --> 00:11:33,590
You may be able to recycle some of your chemicals, depending on what you're working with.

125
00:11:33,590 --> 00:11:39,590
But never wash explosive residues down a drain or schedule them for recycling.

126
00:11:39,590 --> 00:11:44,590
Consult your supervisor regarding how to dispose of these wastes.

127
00:11:44,590 --> 00:11:48,590
More than likely, you'll need to package up your waste for disposal.

128
00:11:48,590 --> 00:11:54,590
Waste containers should be properly labeled and placed in a separate area for pickup.

129
00:11:54,590 --> 00:12:00,590
You also need to ask your supervisor or safety officer about federal and local disposal regulations.

130
00:12:00,590 --> 00:12:06,590
Your facility has a complete plan on how to dispose of chemical waste, so read it.

131
00:12:07,590 --> 00:12:13,590
Remember, you're the one who is ultimately responsible for the disposal arrangements for the materials that you use.

132
00:12:13,590 --> 00:12:27,370
So be sure you know what you're doing.

133
00:12:27,370 --> 00:12:34,370
When you're working with flammable and explosive materials, it's especially important to know what to do in case of an emergency.

134
00:12:34,370 --> 00:12:38,370
To start, read through your facility's emergency plan.

135
00:12:38,370 --> 00:12:42,370
Create contingency plans for spill situations.

136
00:12:42,370 --> 00:12:45,370
Remember that flammable liquids spread quickly.

137
00:12:45,370 --> 00:12:49,370
The larger the spill area, the greater the chance of fire.

138
00:12:49,370 --> 00:12:54,370
So all ignition sources in the lab should be turned off.

139
00:12:54,370 --> 00:12:58,370
Keep spill cleanup equipment and materials nearby.

140
00:12:58,370 --> 00:13:01,370
Personal protective equipment is also important.

141
00:13:01,370 --> 00:13:04,370
See your supervisor about respirator training.

142
00:13:04,370 --> 00:13:09,370
Remember, vermiculite absorbance don't keep vapors from rising into the air.

143
00:13:09,370 --> 00:13:15,370
Ask your supervisor about using activated carbon absorbance, some of which combat this problem.

144
00:13:15,370 --> 00:13:22,620
When you're working with flammables and explosives, you should always think about what you should do in case of an emergency.

145
00:13:22,620 --> 00:13:25,620
Re-read your facility's emergency plan.

146
00:13:25,620 --> 00:13:33,620
Know the locations of fire extinguishers and emergency safety equipment, but only use them if you've been properly trained to do so.

147
00:13:34,620 --> 00:13:37,620
Study evacuation maps and know where your exits are.

148
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:43,620
With a fire or explosion, evacuating your lab may be the only thing you can do.

149
00:13:43,620 --> 00:13:59,960
Because they're always dangerous, working safely with flammables and explosives requires you to know what you're doing and maintain your focus at all times.

150
00:13:59,960 --> 00:14:01,960
Let's review.

151
00:14:01,960 --> 00:14:09,960
Know which of the materials you work with are flammable or explosive and what their characteristics are.

152
00:14:09,960 --> 00:14:17,960
When you're working with flammables and explosives, make sure there are no potential ignition sources nearby.

153
00:14:17,960 --> 00:14:23,960
Be sure you have enough ventilation to remove volatile fumes and vapors from your work area.

154
00:14:23,960 --> 00:14:30,960
Know how the flammable and explosive materials you work with should be handled and stored.

155
00:14:30,960 --> 00:14:35,960
And be prepared in the event a flammable or explosive emergency occurs.

156
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:48,720
By being on your toes and taking appropriate safety measures, you'll be able to work with flammables and explosive materials safely and securely.
